  "statement": "The historical analogy equating the current artificial intelligence build-out to nineteenth-century electrification ignores a basic structural difference in how physical power works versus how proprietary computing works. Nineteenth-century grids distributed a standardized commodity where generation was decoupled from the local appliance, whereas current hardware and training pipelines lock every major capability advance inside a proprietary vault controlled by three cloud empires. When accelerationists argue that open-weight distribution acts as an equalizer, they confuse the consumption of a downstream artifact with the ownership of the upstream means of production. Downloading a compressed parameter file to run on a local workstation does not challenge the underlying concentration of capital, because the model weights themselves were forged in a two-hundred-billion-dollar furnace that only three corporate balance sheets could ignite.\n\nThis separation between downstream inference access and upstream frontier training concentration reveals the fundamental flaw in treating open-source releases as a structural antidote to monopoly power. While hobbyists and small startups can fine-tuning existing weights at the edge, they remain entirely dependent on the hyperscale cloud providers for the specialized compute clusters required to discover the next generation of architectures. The market is not decentralizing; rather, the dominant firms are outsourcing the least profitable tier of distribution and consumer support to the public while retaining absolute ownership over the capital-intensive core engine. A system where three firms dictate the parameters of every frontier model while the rest of the market fights over scraps of open-weight inference is not a decentralized ecosystem.\n\nWhile this industrial consolidation accelerates, local electric utilities in northern Virginia and central Oregon are actively delaying residential green energy transitions to supply gigawatts of continuous power to these server farms. The physical cost of running these training runs is externalized onto local ratepayers through rising utility bills and strained water systems, while the financial windfalls remain concentrated among hardware-owning incumbents. Claiming that edge deployment democratizes technology ignores who pays for the transmission lines and substations feeding the data centers. If open-weight distribution supposedly breaks corporate dominance, why do the same three cloud monopolies continue capturing every dollar of infrastructure return while public grids absorb the physical liabilities?",
